You can detect a machine's native endianness by inspecting how a multi-byte value's first byte reads:

```c
uint32_t x = 1;
if (*(uint8_t*)&x == 1)
    printf("little-endian\n");   // low byte (1) is at the lowest address
else
    printf("big-endian\n");

#include <arpa/inet.h>
uint32_t wire_value = htonl(host_value); // host -> network (big-endian) byte order
uint32_t back        = ntohl(wire_value); // network -> host byte order
```

On a big-endian machine, `htonl`/`ntohl` are no-ops (already the right order); on little-endian, they byte-swap. Portable code calls them unconditionally and never assumes which case it's running on.
